1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,833
Welcome to Class 9: Shiny applications on RCC.

2
00:00:03,833 --> 00:00:08,145
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:08,145 --> 00:00:14,852
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:14,852 --> 00:00:17,247
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:17,247 --> 00:00:23,100
After this class, you can: run a small Shiny app inside a Slurm allocation;

6
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:29,370
explain why tunnelled development is different from production hosting; avoid direct exposure of project files;

7
00:00:29,370 --> 00:00:34,805
design a Shiny app that uses curated data rather than browsing the filesystem;

8
00:00:34,805 --> 00:00:38,567
prepare the information needed for a governed vhost request.

9
00:00:38,567 --> 00:00:44,081
Copy the example: Read the job output and use the SSH tunnel shown there.

10
00:00:44,081 --> 00:00:47,625
The Shiny process binds to 127.0.0.1 on the worker.

11
00:00:47,625 --> 00:00:49,988
This is a development pattern only.

12
00:00:49,988 --> 00:00:56,290
It is not a public service and it is not a replacement for the vhost process.

13
00:00:56,290 --> 00:01:02,662
A production Shiny service needs: an identified project owner and technical contact;

14
00:01:02,662 --> 00:01:10,628
authentication through the RCC gateway; project-group authorization; resource limits; application logs; a defined deployment version;

15
00:01:10,628 --> 00:01:15,407
reviewed data access; a retirement date or review date.

16
00:01:15,407 --> 00:01:21,579
A Shiny app should not receive a raw mount of an entire project directory.

17
00:01:21,579 --> 00:01:27,310
Use one of these safer patterns: a read-only SQLite, DuckDB, or PostgreSQL view;

18
00:01:27,310 --> 00:01:33,482
a curated result directory with approved files; opaque file IDs rather than user-supplied paths;

19
00:01:33,482 --> 00:01:37,009
a separate upload staging area for write workflows.

20
00:01:37,009 --> 00:01:39,546
Running Shiny on a login host.

21
00:01:39,546 --> 00:01:42,083
Binding Shiny to every network interface.

22
00:01:42,083 --> 00:01:46,311
Sharing the URL to a worker port without a tunnel.

23
00:01:46,311 --> 00:01:48,848
Letting users type arbitrary filesystem paths.

24
00:01:48,848 --> 00:01:52,230
Using a personal account as the application identity.

25
00:01:52,230 --> 00:01:56,881
Skipping the vhost request because the demo worked for one person.

26
00:01:56,881 --> 00:02:01,189
Run the local example validation: Then run the Shiny development job once,

27
00:02:01,189 --> 00:02:04,779
open it through the tunnel, and stop it with scancel.

28
00:02:04,779 --> 00:02:10,523
The class is complete when you can explain whether your app is a one-person development session

29
00:02:10,523 --> 00:02:12,318
or a governed project service.

